---
// v1.1 redesign: the About page. Real content landed in a prior session;
// this pass and the one before it are layout-only — the copy below is
// byte-for-byte what shipped originally, not touched again here.
//
// Fixes this pass, all from owner review of the previous layout:
// - The text/photo intro used a plain two-column grid (text | photo) with
//   the body paragraphs as a separate block AFTER it — since the grid row
//   sized to its tallest item (the photo, ~460px, far taller than the
//   short title+standfirst), the body paragraphs couldn't start until
//   that whole row finished, leaving a large dead gap under the
//   standfirst. Fixed with a real CSS Grid row-span: the photo spans two
//   rows (title+standfirst's row, then the body's row) instead of sharing
//   a single row with just the title block — each row's own height is now
//   driven only by what's actually in it (title+standfirst for row one,
//   all the body copy for row two), and the spanning photo just borrows
//   space that's already there across both, rather than dictating row
//   one's height. See the .about__layout comment below for the mechanics.
// - Standfirst: italic, body text size (was a large upright lead
//   paragraph).
// - Everything left-aligned, including the pull-quote and sign-off cards
//   (previously centered).
// - Sign-off: a real avatar circle beside the name/title, one row, not
//   text-only.
// - Hero crop: recentered so both shoulders show symmetrically (previous
//   crop cut off one side) — see the .about__photo object-position
//   comment for how this was actually measured, not guessed.
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Prose from '../components/Prose.astro';
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import { getAuthor } from '../lib/author';
import aboutHero from '../assets/author/about-hero.jpg';

const author = await getAuthor();
---

<Base
  title="About"
  description="Why Cuerpo Coffee exists and who writes it: two decades of paying attention to coffee, starting from a tree on a family plot of land."
>
  <div class="about-band">
    <div class="container about__layout rise-in">
      <div class="about__title-block">
        <h1>About</h1>
        <p class="about__standfirst">
          Fifteen years old, holding a red coffee cherry I didn't know the name for, on my
          father's land. That's where this started.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="about__photo-frame photo-settle">
        <Image
          src={aboutHero}
          alt="Jorge Gomez, founder of Cuerpo Coffee"
          class="about__photo"
          widths={[320, 480, 640]}
          sizes="(min-width: 40rem) 360px, 260px"
          format="avif"
        />
      </div>

      <div class="about__copy">
        <Prose>
          <p>
            I've had a cup of coffee every day since I was fourteen. For most of those years,
            that just meant drinking it. It took a coffee tree to make me actually pay
            attention.
          </p>
          <p>
            My father kept a piece of land outside the city — a small weekend house, and a
            much bigger stretch of trees: guava, lemon, apple, berries, mango, orange,
            potatoes, more flowers than I can name. A few coffee trees were mixed in among
            them. I noticed the red cherries on the branches before I understood what they
            were, and once I did, I couldn't leave it alone.
          </p>
          <p>
            My father walked me through the process himself — picking, pulping, drying, the
            parts most people never see because their coffee already arrives as a bag of
            brown beans. I processed my own batch. Then I bought a small roaster and roasted
            my first kilo. I was fifteen.
          </p>

          <div class="about__pullquote">
            <p class="about__pullquote-text">
              A ritual of drinking coffee, a meditation and a time with yourself.
            </p>
          </div>

          <p>
            That's not a story about becoming a professional. I didn't go work for a roaster,
            and I don't have a certification to point to. What I have is closer to twenty
            years of drinking coffee on purpose — comparing beans, changing variables,
            noticing what actually moves a cup from fine to good.
          </p>
          <p>
            Somewhere in there it stopped being about the caffeine. I built a small coffee
            bar in my own house, bought a real espresso machine and the accessories that go
            with it, and turned the first cup of the morning into something closer to a
            ritual than a habit. I don't drink it to wake up anymore. I drink it to slow down
            before the day asks anything of me.
          </p>
          <p>
            That's the whole premise of this site. Not expertise handed down from a café job,
            but two decades of paying attention, starting from a tree I couldn't identify at
            fifteen. If you're standing where I was — curious, slightly obsessed, not sure
            where to start — that's exactly who this is for.
          </p>
        </Prose>

        <div class="about__signoff">
          {
            author.data.avatar && (
              <Image
                src={author.data.avatar}
                alt={`${author.data.name} photo`}
                width={96}
                height={96}
                class="about__signoff-photo"
              />
            )
          }
          <p class="about__signoff-text">
            <span class="about__signoff-name">{author.data.name}</span> —{' '}
            <span class="about__signoff-title">{author.data.title}</span>
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  /* Same "reading body" white band Article.astro's own body sits on —
     About is a reading page, not a section-banded one. */
  .about-band {
    background: var(--surface-1);
    padding-block: var(--space-8);
  }

  /* Two rows, two columns: [title+standfirst | photo (spans both rows)]
     then [body copy | nothing — the photo already occupies that cell].
     Each row's own height comes only from its own non-spanning content
     (title-block for row one, copy for row two) — the spanning photo just
     fills whatever combined height those two rows already add up to,
     rather than forcing row one to grow to fit it. That combined height
     is always comfortably more than the photo's ~460px once real body
     copy is in row two, which is what actually fixes the dead-gap bug:
     the copy column now starts flowing immediately under the
     title-block, not under the photo. */
  .about__layout {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 360px;
    grid-template-rows: auto 1fr;
    column-gap: var(--space-7);
  }

  .about__title-block {
    grid-column: 1;
    grid-row: 1;
    max-width: var(--measure);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-6);
  }

  .about__copy {
    grid-column: 1;
    grid-row: 2;
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }

  .about__standfirst {
    font-style: italic;
    font-size: var(--step-0);
    color: var(--espresso);
    margin-top: var(--space-4);
  }

  /* The corner portrait: a fixed small photo, not a full-width hero space
     — about-hero.jpg is a face-and-shoulders crop, the wrong shape for
     the landscape treatment Article.astro's own hero uses. The 4:5 ratio
     and radius live on this wrapping frame, not the <img> itself — Astro's
     <Image> sets the img's HTML width/height attributes to the source
     file's own natural size (1170x1278) since no explicit width/height
     was requested, and confirmed directly (computed styles before/after)
     that a browser resolves the img's rendered box from those attributes
     ahead of a CSS `aspect-ratio` declared on that same element. A plain
     wrapping div has no competing intrinsic size of its own, so the ratio
     and clip apply the same way regardless of that interaction. */
  .about__photo-frame {
    grid-column: 2;
    grid-row: 1 / 3;
    width: 100%;
    aspect-ratio: 4 / 5;
    border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
    overflow: hidden;
  }

  /* object-position, not the default center: measured directly against
     the source file (1170x1278), not eyeballed. The subject isn't
     centered in the raw frame — there's visibly more empty window/sky on
     the right than plant/pillar on the left, so a plain center crop at
     this narrower 4:5 ratio cut into one shoulder while leaving empty
     background on the other. 38% horizontal keeps both shoulders roughly
     symmetric in the cropped frame — confirmed by screenshot, not just by
     this measurement, since "roughly centered" is ultimately a visual
     judgment, not a pixel-exact one. */
  .about__photo {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    object-fit: cover;
    object-position: 38% center;
  }

  /* The pull-quote card: docs/DESIGN-SYSTEM.md's pull-quote treatment
     (olive rule, italic, larger Manrope — see ReflectionArticle.astro's
     own blockquote styling) adapted into a self-contained elevated card
     rather than an in-column rule-bordered block. Left-aligned, like
     everything else on this page now — the rule sits at the same left
     edge as the quote text instead of centered above it.
     No shadow, deliberately — docs/DESIGN-SYSTEM.md's Shape/space/depth
     section is explicit and has no stated exception: "shadow only on
     hover-lift and the search dropdown, never at rest." This card is
     static, not a link or button, so there's no hover state to hang a
     shadow on either — adding one anyway (or inventing a hover just to
     justify one) would be the kind of one-off exception that erodes a
     restraint rule the moment it's convenient. The card still reads as a
     deliberate pause without one: a different surface color, generous
     padding, and rounded corners already separate it from the paragraphs
     around it, the same way every other at-rest card on this site (the
     author box, a pillar tile, the newsletter card) achieves elevation
     without a shadow. */
  .about__pullquote {
    /* margin-bottom only — this div is a direct child of <Prose>, so
       Prose's own `.prose > * + *` rule already gives it a margin-top;
       adding one here too would stack the two, the exact bug class
       flagged repeatedly elsewhere in this codebase. */
    background: var(--surface-2);
    border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
    padding: var(--space-7) var(--space-6);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-8);
  }

  .about__pullquote::before {
    content: '';
    display: block;
    width: 3rem;
    height: 2px;
    background: var(--olive-text);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-4);
  }

  .about__pullquote-text {
    font-style: italic;
    font-size: var(--step-2);
    line-height: 1.4;
    color: var(--ink);
    max-width: 32ch;
  }

  /* The closing sign-off — its own card, not a reuse of <AuthorBox />.
     AuthorBox's "Written by" framing fits introducing the author on
     somebody else's reading (an article they didn't write); on the page
     that IS the author's own bio, that label doesn't make sense, so this
     is a purpose-built row instead: avatar, then the name/title as one
     line with an em dash, exactly the sign-off copy's own original
     format. Same surface-2/radius-lg card language as the pull-quote
     above it, so the two read as the page's two deliberate pauses
     without being the same component. */
  .about__signoff {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--space-4);
    background: var(--surface-2);
    border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
    padding: var(--space-6);
    margin-top: var(--space-8);
  }

  .about__signoff-photo {
    flex-shrink: 0;
    width: 3.5rem;
    height: 3.5rem;
    border-radius: var(--radius-pill);
    object-fit: cover;
  }

  .about__signoff-text {
    font-size: var(--step-0);
  }

  .about__signoff-name {
    font-weight: 700;
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  .about__signoff-title {
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  @media (max-width: 40rem) {
    /* Stacked, not full-width: the photo keeps its own modest size and
       centers above the text, rather than growing to fill the mobile
       viewport the way a real hero photo space would. The row-span trick
       is a desktop-only concern (there's no second column to span into
       here), so this simply reverts to a single column, each block in
       normal document order — photo, then title, then copy. */
    .about__layout {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      grid-template-rows: auto auto auto;
    }

    .about__photo-frame {
      grid-column: 1;
      grid-row: 1;
      width: 13rem;
      margin-inline: auto;
      margin-bottom: var(--space-6);
    }

    .about__title-block {
      grid-column: 1;
      grid-row: 2;
    }

    .about__copy {
      grid-column: 1;
      grid-row: 3;
    }
  }
</style>
